===Etymology 2===
{{suffix|en|line|er}} (from the noun).

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A large [[passenger]]-[[carrying]] [[ship]], especially one on a regular [[route]]; an [[ocean liner]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}
|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=Foreword|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=He turned back to the scene before him and the enormous new block of council dwellings. The design was some way after Corbusier but the block was built up on plinths and resembled an Atlantic '''liner''' swimming diagonally across the site.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[ship of the line]].
# {{lb|en|baseball}} A [[line drive]].
#: ''The [[liner]] glanced off the pitcher's foot.''
# {{lb|en|marketing|slang}} A basic [[salesperson]].
# {{lb|en|in combination}} Something with a specified number of lines.
#* '''2005''', G. J. H. Van Gelder, ''Close Relationships'' (page 130)
#*: the following '''three-liner''' by an unknown poet
# {{lb|en|South Korean|_|idol fandom}} person born in a certain year ({{m|en||XX '''liner'''}})
#: {{ux|en|94 '''liner'''}}
# {{short for|en|penny-a-liner}}

